{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-95811",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=90$ and $p=0.47.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =90\\cdot 0.47 \\\\  & =42.3 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =90\\cdot (1-0.47) \\\\  & =47.7 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(90)(0.47)=42.3\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(90)(0.47)(1-0.47)=22.419.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(42.3,22.419).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(40\\le X<50).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(40\\le X<50)=P(40\\le X\\le 49)≈P(39.5\\le Y\\le 49.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(39.5\\le Y\\le 49.5) & =P(\\frac{39.5-42.3}{\\sqrt{22.419}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{49.5-42.3}{\\sqrt{22.419}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.59\\le Z\\le 1.52) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.52)-P(Z<-0.59) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.52)-P(Z\\le -0.59) \\\\  & =Φ(1.52)-Φ(-0.59) \\\\  & =0.9357-0.2776 \\\\  & =0.6581\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(40\\le X<50)≈0.658.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.6587.$",
